 "verdict": [
  "AO-JEPA was skipped in this run, so the drum-JEPA vs AO-JEPA comparison E5 exists for is not answered here; drum-JEPA's linear onset macro-F1 is 0.648 [0.627, 0.664].",
  "Against the controls the trained encoder beats both: random 0.343, raw_mel 0.491, trivial class-prior baseline 0.131; the best representation overall is drumjepa at 0.648, so the same warning as E3 applies \u2014 a probe only counts when it beats the spectrum it was computed from.",
  "What is recoverable is which classes were struck, and only for the frequent, spectrally distinct ones: kick, ride, rimshot score highest and crash2, crash1, crossstick lowest under the drum-JEPA linear probe, tracking how often the class occurs rather than anything about the model; and mean-pooling the 16 frequency tokens of a step throws content away \u2014 concatenating them instead lifts drum-JEPA from 0.629 to 0.644 on the same clips, so every pooled number above is a lower bound.",
  "What is not recoverable is the fine structure of the hit: velocity MAE is 16.9 MIDI units against 24.5 for predicting the per-class training mean, first-onset timing inside the 250 ms step is 38.4 ms against 60.3 ms, and the hi-hat pedal position is 0.123 against 0.181 (CC4/127) \u2014 every one of those beats its baseline, but by 10-25%, so the probes read the event far more sharply than its dynamics or its position inside the step.",
  "Held-out kits cost the encoders little \u2014 drumjepa 0.316 against 0.648, random 0.332 against 0.343 \u2014 but cost the raw log-mel most of its lead (0.284 against 0.491), so the spectrum's advantage is largely kit-specific while what the encoders carry about the action is not."
 ]
}